# E0018: `mut self` on a method

A method of a class or an enum takes `self`, never `mut self`, and a method an interface requires cannot take `mut self` either.

## Why

An object is shared, so `self` can already write every `mut` field of the class. A `mut self` would suggest that the method could replace the object, which no method can do. An enum value never changes, so `mut self` on an enum method has nothing to mean; the help is to return a new value. Removing the `mut` is an unsafe fix there, since the body may still assign `self`.

An interface method has no body, so it cannot change anything. A class that conforms to the interface changes its object's `mut` fields through plain `self`, so the requirement is `fn bump(self)`. Removing the `mut` is a safe fix when `self` is the whole parameter, as in a class.

## Broken

```sisu
class Counter {
    mut n: i64

    fn bump(mut self) {
        self.n += 1
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = Counter(n: 0)
    c.bump()
    print(c.n)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Counter {
    mut n: i64

    fn bump(self) {
        self.n += 1
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = Counter(n: 0)
    c.bump()
    print(c.n)
}
```
